---
import SaidLayout from "../../layouts/SaidLayout.astro";
import SaidPlaceholder from "../../components/SaidPlaceholder.astro";
---

<SaidLayout
  title="The tour — Said by PITO"
  description="Every feature of Said, explained: projects from folders, the board, the dossier, references, meaning search, Claude at the desk, the remote client, and backups."
  active="tour"
>
  <header class="said-hero said-wrap" style="min-height: 56svh;">
    <div class="said-eyebrow said-rise">The tour</div>
    <h1 class="said-rise said-rise-2" style="font-size: clamp(3rem,8vw,6rem);">
      Every feature,<br />plainly said<span class="dot">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="said-hero-sub said-rise said-rise-3">
      No mystery meat. This is what Said does, one frame per feature, in the
      order you'll meet them.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="said-frame">
    <div class="said-wrap said-reveal">
      <p class="said-kicker">01 · Projects</p>
      <h2>Projects are folders.</h2>
      <p>
        You pick one working folder at first boot. Every top-level folder inside
        it becomes a project; new folders become projects on the next scan.
        There is no setup ceremony and no import — your structure is the
        structure.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Each project carries a short key — <code>STU</code>, <code>WORK</code> —
          and numbers its cards for life: <code>STU-42</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          A card keeps its name across every lane and board move inside its
          project.
        </li>
        <li>Loose files at the root belong to no project — folders decide.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="said-visual">
        <SaidPlaceholder label="Screen — the project switcher" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="said-frame">
    <div class="said-wrap said-reveal">
      <p class="said-kicker">02 · The board</p>
      <h2>The board is for deciding.</h2>
      <p>
        Four lanes — backlog, todo, in progress, done — and a card is either
        moving or resting. Done is not one thing: a card resolves as done,
        abandoned, or not relevant, and the record says which.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Keyboard first: every flow rides without the mouse, and the mouse can
          do everything the keys can.
        </li>
        <li>
          Resolved cards file themselves away after a week — nothing is deleted,
          the archive is one filter away.
        </li>
        <li>Every move is history: who, when, from where, to where.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="said-visual">
        <SaidPlaceholder label="Screen — the board, four lanes" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="said-frame">
    <div class="said-wrap said-reveal">
      <p class="said-kicker">03 · The dossier</p>
      <h2>The dossier is for understanding.</h2>
      <p>
        Flip the desk and the same cards become one running record, freshest
        first. Click any card and it opens as a case file: what state it's in,
        how long it lived, who spoke in it, how the spec changed, and every
        journey back from done — called out, not buried.
      </p>
      <div class="said-duo">
        <div>
          <SaidPlaceholder label="Screen — the dossier" ratio="4 / 3" />
        </div>
        <div>
          <SaidPlaceholder label="Screen — a case file" ratio="4 / 3" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="said-frame">
    <div class="said-wrap said-reveal">
      <p class="said-kicker">04 · References</p>
      <h2>Cards point at real lines.</h2>
      <p>
        A card can reference any number of your markdown files, each with its
        own line ranges. The referenced text is readable in place — the card and
        the thinking behind it stay one keystroke apart.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Many cards may reference the same file; a file may feed many cards.
        </li>
        <li>
          New cards get a reference suggested automatically from the project's
          own folder.
        </li>
        <li>Your files are never edited, never moved, never touched.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="said-visual">
        <SaidPlaceholder label="Screen — a reference, read in place" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="said-frame">
    <div class="said-wrap said-reveal">
      <p class="said-kicker">05 · Search</p>
      <h2>Search that gets what you meant.</h2>
      <p>
        Exact-word search covers titles, bodies, and the text inside every
        referenced range. Beside it, an on-device meaning search — embeddings
        computed on your own GPU — finds the note that says it differently.
        Nothing leaves your machine to do it.
      </p>
      <div class="said-visual">
        <SaidPlaceholder label="Screen — the finder, both legs" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="said-frame">
    <div class="said-wrap said-reveal">
      <p class="said-kicker">06 · Claude</p>
      <h2>Claude at the desk.</h2>
      <p>
        Said speaks MCP. A Claude session can list your boards, search your
        notes, create and move cards, comment in threads, and attach captures —
        as itself. Every action carries the agent's name in the history and in
        the thread, exactly like yours carries yours.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>One command connects it: <code>claude mcp add pito-work</code>.</li>
        <li>
          Agents collaborate on cards; several can work the same card at once.
        </li>
        <li>It needs your key, and it never touches a board unasked.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="said-visual">
        <SaidPlaceholder label="Screen — a card thread with named agents" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="said-frame">
    <div class="said-wrap said-reveal">
      <p class="said-kicker">07 · Remote</p>
      <h2>The desk in your pocket.</h2>
      <p>
        A remote client rides the same desk from your phone, through your own
        tunnel, with one token. The window closed means the remote is off —
        there is no daemon, no always-on server, no surprise surface.
      </p>
      <div class="said-visual">
        <SaidPlaceholder
          label="Screen — the remote client, mobile"
          ratio="9 / 16"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="said-frame">
    <div class="said-wrap said-reveal">
      <p class="said-kicker">08 · Backups</p>
      <h2>Backed up before you asked.</h2>
      <p>
        Once a day while it's open, Said backs up its database and every
        attachment to the folder you chose — keeping two weeks of history. Your
        notes aren't its to back up: those stay your files, in your hands, on
        your terms.
      </p>
      <div class="said-visual">
        <SaidPlaceholder label="Screen — jobs, back up now" />
      </div>
      <div class="said-cta-row" style="margin-top: 34px;">
        <a class="said-btn said-btn-primary" href="/said/guides/"
          >Now read the guides</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</SaidLayout>
